import { WebReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> WebReaderCopy = WebReaderCopy {
  summary: "Describe a button’s label, disabled state and event payload.",
  taskTitle: "Separate button markup from click behavior",
  purpose: "First serialize a button labelled “Show details” in enabled and disabled forms. This is a static example. The resulting string alone cannot toggle the release details when clicked.",
  setup: "fn control accepts disabled: Bool and returns Html<Unit>. Bool is False or True. Writing disabled by itself in the record abbreviates disabled: disabled. onClick: () supplies a Unit value for the event payload. The { ... } supplied to button is a record of named fields, like a TS object.",
  after: [
    "children supplies the label. ariaExpanded: False becomes aria-expanded=\"false\"; disabled: False emits no disabled attribute. control True adds the disabled attribute. Omitting buttonType gives type=\"button\".",
    "do in pub effect fn main runs the println operations in order. renderToString omits onClick, so neither output includes the event payload or JavaScript."
  ],
  details: [
    "In the declaration, Action is the type of value a later event may send; C is the children input type. where IntoChildren<C, Action> requires that input to be convertible to ordered children. Here Action is Unit and C is String.",
    "onClick here is the value (), not a function being called during construction. The browser continuation uses the value ToggleDetails and lets app pass events to update. Serializing static markup does not install that behavior.",
    "ariaExpanded communicates expansion state; it does not hide the paragraph. The app example derives ariaExpanded, the paragraph’s hidden field and the button label from the same Bool to keep them consistent.",
    "Give the button a label that identifies its action. Correct types do not by themselves establish suitable labels or accessibility across the whole page."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS can also set a button’s disabled and aria-expanded values. Registering a click handler with addEventListener is a separate operation from creating an HTML string.",
  comparisonAfter: "Seseragi likewise separates pure button construction from browser event handling. The complete details-toggle example is on app below."
}
